{"event_id": "ea_HESM_Q2_2026", "ticker": "HESM", "event_datetime": "2026-08-03T14:00:00+00:00", "car1": -0.016759695379563233, "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Q2 profitability exceeded the company's flat-quarter guidance: net income of $174 million was $14 million above the top of the $150\u2013160 million range, and adjusted EBITDA of $314 million was $9 million above the $295\u2013305 million range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Q2 2026 net income was $174 million, up from $158 million in Q1 2026, while adjusted EBITDA rose to $314 million from $300 million.", "expectation_evidence": "Company guidance for Q2 ... net income $150\u2013160M, Adjusted EBITDA $295\u2013305M \u2014 explicitly \"flat with the first quarter.\"", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The Q2 gross adjusted EBITDA margin reached approximately 85%, 10 percentage points above the company's 75% target. The outperformance was partly temporary: operating expenses shifted into the second half, and minor one-time credits helped the quarter.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "The company's gross adjusted EBITDA margin reached approximately 85% in Q2 2026, well above its 75% target, aided partly by OpEx phasing shifts into the second half and minor one-time credits.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "call"}, {"description": "Gas processing volumes increased slightly to 433 MMcf/d from Q1's 430 MMcf/d despite the expected Tioga maintenance headwind. Additional third-party volumes supplied the upside that the preview identified as a possible route to an earnings beat.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Q2 2026 throughput averaged 433 MMcf/d for gas processing ... with gas volumes boosted by additional third-party volumes.", "expectation_evidence": "Q2 carries the Tioga outage ... planned Tioga Gas Plant maintenance of 5\u201310 MMcf/d. ... A clean beat likely requires the Tioga outage to have come in at the light end ... or third-party volume upside.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The capital-allocation update showed no Q2 buybacks, following $60 million of repurchases in Q1; instead, Hess Midstream repaid $87 million of revolver debt. This was a shift toward debt reduction rather than continued repurchases, although the preview did not specify a Q2 buyback amount.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company did not execute a share buyback in Q2 2026, instead paying down $87 million on its revolving credit facility, reducing the drawn balance to $256 million.", "expectation_evidence": "Capital returns: $60M of Class A share + Class B unit repurchases ... the incremental news is buyback pace and whether another sponsor (Chevron) unit repurchase is executed. ... Watch ... the split between repurchases and debt.", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Full-year net income guidance of $650\u2013700 million and adjusted EBITDA guidance of $1.225\u20131.275 billion were reaffirmed, matching the preview's base case despite the Q2 earnings beat.", "Full-year adjusted free cash flow guidance remained $910\u2013960 million; the additional increase contemplated in the preview's bull case did not occur.", "Q2 adjusted free cash flow declined approximately 2% sequentially to $232 million, consistent with the expected sequential decline.", "Management reiterated the second-half volume ramp, expecting at least 5% volume growth rather than signaling the deterioration contemplated in the bear case.", "The approximately $280 million of excess adjusted free cash flow after targeted 5% annual distribution growth, and the leverage path from approximately 3.0x toward 2.5x by 2028, matched the preview.", "Commentary that Chevron can sustain production at lower rig counts supported the preview's expectation of production resilience despite reduced drilling activity."]}
{"event_id": "ea_MUSA_Q2_2026", "ticker": "MUSA", "event_datetime": "2026-08-06T15:00:00+00:00", "car1": -0.07551982073350261, "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Murphy's controllable fuel-supply earnings exceeded 7 cents per gallon, far above the preview's roughly 2.5-cent core assumption. Supply constraints benefited its assets rather than leaving the business solely exposed to the inventory losses the preview emphasized; the actual inventory revaluation and total supply contribution were not provided.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "The controllables portion of the fuel supply business earned over $0.07 per gallon this quarter ... reflecting the advantage of Murphy's supply assets during the current supply-constrained environment.", "expectation_evidence": "My est.: +2 cpg (core ~2.5, mark ~-0.5 to -3).", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management's second-half planning assumption was approximately 35 cents per gallon all-in, a more cautious forward margin outlook than the preview's expectation of sustained elevated margins and potential further expansion. Management explicitly called this conservative, with upside from prolonged price declines; it is not directly comparable to the preview's retail-only margin figures.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management guides to an all-in fuel margin of approximately $0.35 per gallon for the back half of the year, which they characterized as intentionally conservative with upside if prices fall for an extended period.", "expectation_evidence": "The market is paying 19x forward for a sustained elevated-margin regime. Q3 started with gasoline prices expected down ~41 cpg vs. Q2 (EIA), which historically supports further margin expansion.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management expects conflict-related fuel-supply tightness to last well into next year, extending the favorable backdrop for Murphy's supply assets beyond the preview's ceasefire-and-resumption narrative.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management believes fuel supply tightness from the geopolitical conflict will not normalize until well into next year, and does not expect a quick resolution.", "expectation_evidence": "Brent hit $118/bbl on April 29 (Strait of Hormuz disruption), then collapsed to $72/bbl by June 26 on ceasefire/resumption-of-traffic news.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Full-year merchandise contribution is now expected near the bottom of the $890\u2013900 million range rather than simply remaining on track. Management cited consumer budget pressure and winter-storm closures affecting up to half the network.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Merchandise contribution is expected to land toward the low end of the guidance range due to consumer budget pressures and Q1 weather impacts that closed up to half the network during winter storms.", "expectation_evidence": "FY guide $890\u2013900M; Q1 did $210.2M (+7.3%) at 20.0% margin.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "New-store deliveries are tracking toward approximately 45, the bottom of the 45\u201355 guidance range, rather than an undifferentiated on-track outcome. The upper end depended on tuck-in acquisitions that are not currently in the pipeline.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "New store (NTI) deliveries will come in closer to 45 units at the lower end of guidance, as the high end assumed tuck-in acquisitions that are not currently in the pipeline.", "expectation_evidence": "45\u201355 NTIs + up to 30 raze-and-rebuilds guided for FY26 ... My est.: On track.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Capital spending is trending toward the top of the $475\u2013525 million range even as current-year store deliveries trend toward the bottom of their range. Spending includes accelerated 2027 construction, life-cycle investments and land purchases, supporting future growth but using cash ahead of stores' roughly three-year profitability ramp.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "CapEx is trending to the high end of the range due to pulling forward 2027 store construction, proactive life-cycle investments, and land bank investment, though new stores take about 3 years to reach full profitability ramp.", "expectation_evidence": "Capex $475\u2013525M. ... My est.: On track.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "May fuel volumes rose more than 2%, contrary to the preview's expectation of April/May demand destruction. The quarter's limited exposure to pump prices above $4 and the strong response to May's wholesale-price decline show a more favorable demand pattern than anticipated.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "In May, when RBOB fell 16-18%, same-store volume rose over 2%. ... Stores [were] priced above $4 only 18% of the time.", "expectation_evidence": "April/May $4+ pump prices = demand destruction; June collapse = tailwind.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The elevated loyalty acquisition pace persisted throughout Q2, with sign-ups exceeding 600,000 each month rather than being confined to March's previously highlighted milestone. Nearly 46% of sign-ups were new or lapsed customers, versus 40% in Q1, adding evidence of broader customer recruitment.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Loyalty program sign-ups exceeded $600,000 per month every month in Q2 (up from ~$400,000 previously), with new or lapsed customers rising to nearly 46% of signees from 40% in Q1.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["Q2 same-store fuel volume growth of 0.5% fell within the preview's flat-to-1.5% growth estimate.", "Fuel margins in the high 30s cents per gallon during the first five days of August supported the preview's expectation that early-Q3 margins would remain elevated, although the call fact does not specify retail-only versus all-in margins.", "Management reiterated the tough Q3 nicotine promotional comparison flagged in the preview, while expecting nicotine to remain a second-half tailwind through Cowboy Cut cigarettes and pouch growth."]}
{"event_id": "ea_TNON_Q2_2026", "ticker": "TNON", "event_datetime": "2026-08-13T20:30:00+00:00", "car1": -0.13305722773210937, "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Q2 revenue of $1.3 million missed the $1.54 million consensus by approximately 16% and declined from Q1's $1.38 million rather than increasing as expected. First-half revenue of $2.7 million also fell short of the preview's approximately $2.92 million, leaving a larger second-half ramp needed to reach the previously stated annual targets.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 2026 revenue was $1.3 million, up 127% year-over-year\u201d; \u201cFirst half 2026 revenue reached $2.7 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cRevenue | $1.54M\u201d; \u201cQ1 FY2026 Actual | $1.38M\u201d; \u201cIn-line H1 (~$2.92M) leaves $3.5\u20133.67M in H2 to hit guidance/consensus.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 gross margin was 64%, approximately four percentage points below the expected 68% and below the preview's explicit 65% downside threshold. Despite substantial year-over-year improvement, margin moved backward from Q1's 68.5% rather than toward management's greater-than-70% objective.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 gross margin expanded to 64% from 43% a year ago\u201d; \u201cgross profit up 232% to $0.8 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cGross margin | ~68% (est.)\u201d; \u201cManagement guided to continued expansion past 70%. A slip below 65% on mix or inventory charges would remove the one unambiguously positive trend.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The split-adjusted loss of $12.35 per share was approximately 47% larger than the expected $8.40 loss, while the $4.1 million net loss exceeded the expected $2.9\u20133.0 million. The reported loss included $0.9 million of non-cash interest from convertible-note amortization, a specific earnings burden the preview had not quantified.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 net loss widened to $4.1 million ($12.35 per share) ... partly driven by $0.9 million of non-cash interest expense from convertible note amortization.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cEPS (post-split basis) | -$8.40\u201d; \u201cImplied net loss | ~$2.9\u20133.0M\u201d; \u201cOnly the split-adjusted figure is comparable to reported EPS.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "First-half revenue and gross-profit gains did not translate into a smaller operating loss: the operating loss remained approximately $6.6 million versus $6.5 million a year earlier. This adds a caution to the preview's operating-leverage thesis, although the preview supplied no first-half operating-loss forecast.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDespite revenue doubling and gross profit roughly tripling, six-month operating loss was essentially flat at $6.6 million versus $6.5 million a year ago.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "The July Catamaran clearance brought a specific cost benefit beyond the clearance already anticipated in the preview: certain instruments can now be reused rather than discarded. Management expects lower per-procedure costs and a margin benefit beginning in Q3.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFDA 510(k) clearance for the Catamaran SI joint fusion system, reclassifying certain instruments from disposable to reusable, which is expected to reduce per-procedure costs and boost margins beginning in Q3.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cCatamaran received updated 510(k) clearance July 2.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Training activity nearly doubled in the first half versus the preceding six months, providing a new quantified indication of the Tampa facility's contribution. The preview anticipated a facility ramp but did not provide a training-event growth target.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cPhysician and distributor training events increased 98% in the first half of 2026 versus the second half of 2025, driven largely by the newly opened Tampa training facility.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201c21 physicians trained in Q1; Tampa training facility ramping.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management reported a record case-value month in July, adding an encouraging early second-half commercial signal where the preview had questioned the visibility of the required ramp. No July target or dollar value was provided, so this does not establish that the annual revenue bridge is covered.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cManagement reported a record case value month in July.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cReaffirming +63% without a visible driver is a soft promise; the ramp depends on converting trained surgeons into repeat users through hospital value-analysis committees.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["June 30 cash of $1.7 million was within the preview's expected $1.5\u20132.5 million range; the $4.2 million July 1 offering was also already anticipated as a subsequent event.", "Q2 operating expenses of $4.2 million matched management's stated baseline and stayed below the preview's approximately $4.4 million warning threshold. Year-over-year expense growth was also below revenue growth.", "Continued progress toward multiple product launches was consistent with the preview's pipeline expectations, including the enhanced Symmetry Plus system and a new posterior-approach technology."]}
{"event_id": "ea_HYPD_Q2_2026", "ticker": "HYPD", "event_datetime": "2026-08-12T21:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.04557576227260851, "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Net income of $31.0 million fell below the preview's $35\u201347 million estimate, despite treasury gains exceeding the modeled range. The supplied facts do not explain the full earnings shortfall.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Q2 FY2026 net income hit a record $31.0 million ... driven largely by $54.8 million in treasury gains.", "expectation_evidence": "GAAP net income: ~$35\u201347M; Unrealized gain on digital assets: +$38\u201350M.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "HYPE's $65 quarter-end price exceeded the modeled $55\u201362 range, lifting treasury value to $133 million versus $112\u2013125 million expected and NAV to $134 million versus $110\u2013120 million. Roughly $52 million of unrealized gains also slightly exceeded the $38\u201350 million estimate.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company's hype treasury reached $133 million (2.04 million tokens at a $65.0 quarter-end price) ... producing roughly $52 million of unrealized gains; net asset value nearly doubled to $134 million.", "expectation_evidence": "HYPE price, period end: ~$55\u201362; Gross HYPE holdings: ~$112\u2013125M; NAV: ~$110\u2013120M; Unrealized gain on digital assets: +$38\u201350M.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management reported redeploying 1 million HYPE into two Q3 partnerships, rather than relying only on the disclosed 500,000-token Skew agreement while leaving 300,000 tokens without a disclosed home. The new arrangements also brought improved economics and rights to 5% of SKU's equity and tokens, materially easing the preview's concern about idle capital and Q3 monetization.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "Within weeks of the USDH headwind, the company redeployed 1 million hype into new Q3 house partnerships with SKU (HIP4) and Entropy (HIP3), securing improved economics plus a right to 5% of SKU's equity and 5% of its tokens.", "expectation_evidence": "The replacement \u2014 the Skew Technologies HAUS agreement for 500K HYPE \u2014 was not signed until July 15. That leaves roughly six weeks of idle capital spanning late Q2 and early Q3, and 300K HYPE still without a disclosed home. ... Q3 is where the drag bites.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Operating expenses excluding stock compensation were $2.3 million, below the preview's $2.8\u20133.1 million estimate. Management also forecast another 10\u201320% reduction after the legacy biotech runoff, extending the cost-saving outlook beyond the preview's general expectation of declining expenses.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Operating expenses excluding stock-based comp fell 21% quarter-over-quarter to $2.3 million ... with management guiding to a further 10\u201320% reduction after fully running off the legacy biotech segment.", "expectation_evidence": "Opex: ~$2.8\u20133.1M; declining trend, legacy wind-down.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted EBITDA reached $53.7 million, exceeding the upper end of the preview's $40\u201350 million estimate by $3.7 million.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Adjusted EBITDA jumped to $53.7 million from $19.5 million, driven largely by $54.8 million in treasury gains.", "expectation_evidence": "Adjusted EBITDA: ~$40\u201350M; tracks marks.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "HYPD completed its first institutional credit transaction, adding a lending deployment not anticipated in the preview: $1 million USDC lent at 8% APY against natively staked HYPE, with collateral held at Anchorage Digital.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "The company completed its first institutional credit deal on the Avia platform, lending $1 million USDC against natively staked hype at 8% APY, with collateral custodied at Anchorage Digital.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Approximately 15.5 million shares were outstanding as of August 10, about 2.3% above the preview's 15.16 million reference count. That is a modest adverse change for the per-share ownership measure the preview emphasized, although the supplied facts do not establish the source of the increase or whether shares were issued below NAV.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Approximately 15.5 million shares outstanding as of August 10.", "expectation_evidence": "Shares out: 15.16M. ... Any ATM issuance during Q2/Q3 below NAV \u2014 check the share count in the 10-Q against 15,157,747.", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Adjusted gross profit of $1.2 million was within the preview's $1.0\u20131.3 million estimate and at the upper end of its $1.0\u20131.2 million base case; the 20% sequential growth was not an earnings surprise.", "Full-year adjusted gross profit guidance was reiterated at $5\u20137 million, with neither a cut nor a narrowing to the low end.", "Management retained its year-end operating cash-flow objective, targeting positive adjusted net operating cash flow rather than postponing the milestone.", "HYPE holdings of 2.04 million tokens were within the expected 2.00\u20132.10 million range, and approximately 11,000 tokens earned during Q2 were close to the preview's roughly 10,000-per-quarter staking assumption.", "Cash, equivalents and stablecoins of $11.8 million were within the preview's $11\u201314 million cash estimate.", "The Native Markets and Felix wind-downs freed 800,000 HYPE, as anticipated. The resulting decline in DeFi monetization revenue to $158,000 confirmed the expected USDH-related headwind, although the preview supplied no revenue estimate for those deployments."]}
{"event_id": "ea_SUNB_Q1_2027", "ticker": "SUNB", "event_datetime": "2026-09-09T12:30:00+00:00", "car1": 0.06454853198626864, "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "SUNB raised full-year guidance rather than delivering the expected reaffirmation. Revenue growth guidance increased to 6%-9% from 4.5%-7.5%, rental growth to 7%-10% from 5%-8%, and adjusted EBITDA to $4.92-$5.12 billion from $4.85-$5.05 billion.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management raised FY2027 guidance to total revenue growth of 6%-9%, rental revenue growth of 7%-10%, and adjusted EBITDA of $4.92 billion-$5.12 billion.", "expectation_evidence": "With one quarter of a four-quarter year in the bank, the base case is a reaffirm.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted EBITDA margin of 42.2% was 230-330 basis points below the preview's estimated range and below its roughly 44% warning threshold. Although the reported 100-basis-point year-over-year decline matched the anticipated scale of deterioration, the absolute margin was substantially weaker than expected.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "Adjusted EBITDA increased 8.7% to $1.3 billion but margin declined to 42.2% from 43.2% a year earlier, though it improved 350 basis points sequentially from Q4.", "expectation_evidence": "Adj. EBITDA margin: ~44.5-45.5% (est.). Anything below ~44% and the \"flat FY margin\" guide becomes arithmetically hard.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted EPS of $1.18 exceeded the $1.04-$1.08 consensus range by approximately 9%-13% and also topped the preview's $1.15 bull case. Higher operating profit and repurchases supported the beat.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Adjusted EPS rose 20.4% to a first-quarter record of $1.18, aided by higher operating profit and share repurchases.", "expectation_evidence": "Adj. EPS: $1.04-1.08. Use $1.06 as the practical hurdle. Bull: $1.15.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Total revenue of $3.1 billion beat the $2.97-$3.00 billion expectation by approximately 3%-4%, while rental revenue of $2.9 billion exceeded the $2.70-$2.73 billion estimate by approximately 6%-7%. Total revenue also surpassed the preview's $3.05 billion bull case.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Q1 FY2027 total revenue grew 11.2% to $3.1 billion and rental revenue grew 12.5% to $2.9 billion, both first-quarter records.", "expectation_evidence": "Total revenue: $2.97-3.00bn. Rental revenue: ~$2.70-2.73bn (est.). Bull revenue: $3.05bn.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Despite the revenue beat, adjusted EBITDA of $1.3 billion fell approximately 2%-4% short of the preview's $1.33-$1.36 billion estimate.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Adjusted EBITDA increased 8.7% to $1.3 billion.", "expectation_evidence": "Adj. EBITDA: ~$1.33-1.36bn (est.).", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "SUNB increased planned fleet investment rather than retaining the existing spending ranges. Gross capex guidance rose by $300 million at each endpoint to $2.75-$3.15 billion, while net rental capex rose by $350 million at each endpoint to $2.4-$2.8 billion, indicating stronger investment demand but greater cash requirements.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Gross capital expenditure guidance was raised to $2.75 billion-$3.15 billion and net rental capex to $2.4 billion-$2.8 billion, driven by mega project, specialty and energy demand.", "expectation_evidence": "Gross rental capex: $2.45-2.85bn. Net rental capex: $2.05-2.45bn.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "General Tool rental growth of 7.4% comfortably exceeded the preview's explicit upside threshold of 4%, contradicting the concern that the segment was stalling.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "General Tool rental revenue grew 7.4% with dollar utilization flat at 47%.", "expectation_evidence": "General tool above +4% would be the genuine upside surprise \u2014 that's the segment the bears say is stalling.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Specialty rental growth reached 25.3%, well above the preview's 15%+ bullish marker and matching the roughly 25% peer growth rate it identified as the competitive benchmark. Dollar utilization of 77%, up 300 basis points, reinforced the strong segment performance.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "North American Specialty rental revenue surged 25.3% with dollar utilization up 300 basis points to 77%.", "expectation_evidence": "Bull: specialty +15%+. Specialty growth vs. URI's +25% \u2014 relative, not absolute. This is the share-shift tell.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The call supplied reassuring evidence on fleet productivity against the preview's concern about ageing assets and weak utilization: rental revenue grew 12.5% with depreciation up only 2.5%, while General Tool same-store fleet grew just about 1%. This supports disciplined fleet deployment, although it does not resolve the unanswered fleet-age and disposal-value questions.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Rental revenue grew 12.5% versus depreciation growth of only 2.5%, and General Tool same-store fleet increased only about 1% year-over-year, signaling disciplined fleet growth without over-fleeting.", "expectation_evidence": "A rising fleet age with flat utilization is how rental companies flatter near-term cash flow and pay for it later in maintenance and used-value realization.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The FIFA World Cup provided an unanticipated, event-specific boost of roughly 250 basis points to quarterly rental revenue growth. The preview did not identify this contributor to the top-line result.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The FIFA World Cup contributed roughly 250 basis points ... to rental revenue growth in the quarter.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "SUNB completed a $1.2 billion senior notes offering at 4.95% and 5.65%, a financing development not anticipated in the preview. The supplied facts do not establish whether this was incremental borrowing or refinancing, so its shareholder implications are unclear.", "direction": "unclear", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company completed a $1.2 billion senior notes offering at 4.95% and 5.65%.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["General Tool dollar utilization remained flat at 47%, consistent with the flat 47% utilization backdrop highlighted in the preview.", "The quarterly dividend transition occurred as expected, with a $0.30 quarterly dividend; the preview did not specify an expected quarterly amount.", "Greenfield expansion remained on track for approximately 55 openings, matching guidance, with 13 opened in the quarter.", "Net leverage of 1.8x remained within the stated 1-2x target range.", "The anticipated acquisition contribution appeared in the results: Aries/ARRIS added approximately 100 basis points to rental revenue growth. The supplied facts do not quantify the margin dilution that the preview wanted management to explain."]}
{"event_id": "ea_QSI_Q2_2026", "ticker": "QSI", "event_datetime": "2026-08-13T20:30:00+00:00", "car1": -0.062373343610114707, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Investors were led to expect a Proteus commercial launch at the end of 2026, with early-access instruments going into sites that summer. Quantum-Si pushed the launch to Q2 2027, said early access has not begun and is now expected in early 2027, and cited an additional instrument-design cycle to de-risk manufacturing.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDelayed its Proteus platform launch from end of 2026 to Q2 2027,\u201d adding \u201can additional integrated instrument design cycle\u201d; the early access program \u201chas not yet begun\u201d and is expected to shift to early 2027.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said management committed to putting instruments into sites \u201cthis summer,\u201d described the expected launch as \u201cat the end of 2026,\u201d and identified a launch slip into 1H27 as the bear case.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview identified 18 amino acids as the launch requirement and expected all 20 to be demonstrated during 2026. Despite the later launch, management now expects Proteus to detect 19 or 20 amino acids at launch and says all 20 will be demonstrated in 2026.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Proteus is now expected to launch with detection of \u201ceither 19 or 20 amino acids,\u201d up from 18 planned, with \u201call 20 to be demonstrated in 2026.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cLaunch requires 18\u201d; \u201call 20 demonstrated during 2026, delivered on Proteus in 2027.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 revenue was above the preview\u2019s $0.30 million consensus and $0.27 million base estimate, although it remained far below the year-earlier quarter. The preview expected weak sales ahead of Proteus, but the reported decline was less severe than its estimates implied.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Q2 2026 revenue was \u201c$344,000 from $591,000 a year earlier.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Q2 revenue consensus was \u201c$0.30M\u201d and the preview\u2019s base estimate was \u201c$0.27M (\u201154% y/y).\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Quantum-Si announced a workforce reduction the preview did not anticipate, with expected annualized operating-expense savings of about $12 million. The savings are not expected to start until the second half of Q4 2026.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company announced a reduction in force of \u201capproximately 20% of total headcount,\u201d expected to generate \u201croughly $12 million in annualized operating expense savings starting in the second half of Q4 2026.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Management extended its stated cash runway beyond the preview\u2019s expected Q2 2028, saying the company can fund operations into Q4 2028 after the cost-cutting actions.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company said its $169.9 million in cash and investments provides runway \u201cinto Q4 2028 after the cost-cutting actions.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview expected \u201crunway into Q2 2028\u201d and said reaffirming Q2 2028 was \u201ctable stakes.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management provided new, sizable Proteus market and prospect figures that the preview had not specified: a more-than-$4 billion annual non-human proteomics opportunity, over 250 institutions expressing interest, and more than 150 AAV gene-therapy companies as potential users. These are opportunity and interest figures, not evidence of completed early-access deployments or purchases.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management estimated the non-human proteomics opportunity at \u201cmore than $4 billion annually\u201d; it had qualified \u201cmore than 250 unique institutions with stated interest in Proteus\u201d and identified \u201cover 150 biotech and pharma companies\u201d developing AAV gene therapies as a potential application market.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["Full-year 2026 guidance was reiterated at approximately $1 million in revenue, adjusted operating expenses of $98 million or less, and total cash usage of $93 million or less, as the preview expected.", "The $169.9 million cash-and-investments balance was within the preview\u2019s expected range of approximately $167\u2013169 million.", "The $23.5 million Q2 net loss was close to the preview\u2019s approximately $24 million estimate."]}
{"event_id": "ea_ALLO_Q2_2026", "ticker": "ALLO", "event_datetime": "2026-08-12T21:00:00+00:00", "car1": -0.04994710826238413, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Allogene said Alpha-3 had already surpassed 80 active sites in July and now expects about 100 by year-end. The preview expected expansion to 80+ sites, so the reported pace\u2014roughly six months ahead of the prior site-activation goal\u2014and higher year-end count are better than expected.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cReached its year-end goal of activating over 80 Alpha-3 clinical sites in July\u201d and \u201cnow expects approximately 100 active sites by year-end\u201d; management said site activation accelerated \u201cby roughly six months.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201c60+ North American sites expanding to 80+ globally\u201d; the preview also described 80+ sites as the expected expansion milestone.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The call offered no patient screening, MRD-positive rate, or randomization counts, leaving the enrollment funnel and screen-to-enroll conversion unquantified despite the site-activation progress. The preview called those figures the most valuable disclosure and said their absence would be mildly negative.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "The call reported site counts and the 220-patient trial target, but no patients screened, MRD-positive, or randomized.", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAny quantification \u2014 patients screened, MRD+ rate, patients randomized \u2014 is the single most valuable disclosure\u201d; \u201cAbsence of numbers is mildly negative by now.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management said statistical significance at Alpha-3\u2019s interim EFS analysis will require overwhelming efficacy because of the alpha allocation between interim and primary analyses. The preview highlighted the mid-2027 interim readout but did not flag this high bar, which makes the interim result harder to achieve than the preview conveyed.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAchieving statistical significance at the interim EFS analysis will require overwhelming efficacy due to how alpha was allocated between the interim and primary analyses.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cGating an interim EFS analysis mid-2027 and primary EFS mid-2028.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The call supplied favorable Alpha-3 interim details: most patients cleared MRD rapidly, there were no treatment-related hospitalizations, and most treatment and follow-up occurred as outpatients, including in community practices without prior CAR-T experience. The preview discussed earlier interim data but did not set an expectation for these specific efficacy and outpatient-delivery details.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The interim futility analysis showed \u201crapid MRD clearance in a majority of patients with no treatment-related hospitalizations,\u201d with most patients treated and followed \u201centirely in the outpatient setting,\u201d including at community practices with no prior CAR-T experience.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Management used the TRANSFORM Breyanzi experience to argue that a 24% MRD-clearance differential corresponded to a greater than 60% EFS improvement, and said Alpha-3\u2019s MRD-clearance results were stronger. That favorable comparison was not part of the preview\u2019s stated expectations.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management cited that \u201cthe TRANSFORM study of Breyanzi showed a 24% MRD clearance differential translated into a greater than 60% EFS improvement,\u201d framing expectations for Alpha-3\u2019s stronger MRD-clearance results.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Allogene reported an optimized ALLO-316 regimen with a 31% confirmed response rate in CD70-high renal cell carcinoma, and none of the five confirmed responders had progressed at follow-up of 8 to over 18 months after one dose. The preview mentioned the trial\u2019s disposition but did not anticipate this efficacy and durability update.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe optimized regimen produced a 31% confirmed overall response rate in CD70-high renal cell carcinoma,\u201d and \u201cnone of the 5 confirmed responders\u201d had progressed at follow-up ranging from 8 to over 18 months after a single dose.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview flagged \u201cALLO-316 disposition (Phase 1 published in JCO, July 2026)\u201d but did not state an expected response rate or durability result.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management took a favorable strategic view of Alpha-3\u2019s competitive protection, saying ongoing first-line CAR-T and bispecific studies should have minimal effect on post-frontline MRD positivity and that in vivo CAR-T is likely limited to relapsed/refractory use. The preview raised new-CEO strategy and partnership intent as questions but did not preview this specific competitive argument.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management expects ongoing first-line CAR-T and bispecific studies to have \u201cminimal impact on post-frontline MRD positivity rates\u201d because of toxicity profiles, and views in vivo CAR-T as likely limited to replacing autologous CAR-T in relapsed/refractory settings, leaving Alpha-3\u2019s community-based niche \u201cwell protected.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview asked investors to watch the new CEO\u2019s \u201cpipeline prioritization\u201d and \u201cBD intent\u201d but did not state a view on competitive threats to Alpha-3.", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "The call stated that the ALLO-329 clinical and translational update would come in Q4 2025, versus the preview\u2019s Q4 2026 expectation. That would be a substantial pull-forward, but Q4 2025 had already passed by the August 2026 release date, making the reported timing internally inconsistent and the actual timing surprise unclear.", "direction": "unclear", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe ALLO-329 RESOLUTION autoimmune trial will report a clinical and translational data update in Q4 2025.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cNext update guided to Q4 2026\u201d; the preview also expected ALLO-329 to \u201cstay Q4.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Management maintained the mid-2027 timing for Alpha-3\u2019s interim EFS analysis, matching the preview\u2019s expected timeline, although the site-activation goal was reached ahead of schedule.", "The FDA\u2019s RMAT and Fast Track designations were already known before the call. Management said they validated MRD positivity as an unmet medical need, but the call facts do not report FDA agreement that MRD clearance can serve as a surrogate endpoint for accelerated approval; the preview\u2019s base case expected RMAT to be framed as an engagement benefit only.", "The call described ALLO-329 as having previously treated nine patients and planned a Q4 update, broadly matching the preview\u2019s expectation of an update after nine patients."]}
{"event_id": "ea_CW_Q2_2026", "ticker": "CW", "event_datetime": "2026-08-06T14:00:00+00:00", "car1": -0.04439630269826722, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Curtiss-Wright raised full-year 2026 sales growth guidance to 8%-9% from 7%-8%, diluted EPS growth to 14%-16% and $15.10-$15.40 from $14.90-$15.30, and operating margin to 19.1%-19.3% from 19.0%-19.2%. The preview identified a guidance raise as important but expected a narrower range or a raise mainly toward the high end; the reported increases lift the low ends of sales and EPS guidance.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFull-year 2026 guidance was raised to 8%-9% sales growth, 14%-16% diluted EPS growth to $15.10-$15.40, and record operating margin of 19.1%-19.3%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cFY26 guide: Sales $3.740\u20133.795B (+7\u20138%); Operating margin 19.0\u201319.2%; Diluted EPS $14.90\u201315.30.\u201d The preview\u2019s base case expected guidance to be \u201cnarrowed to the high end rather than raised outright.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 operating margin reached 19.4%, well above the preview\u2019s estimated 18.5% and the expected second-half-weighted margin expansion. This was a larger Q2 margin result than the preview\u2019s roadmap suggested.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cOperating margin expanded 110 basis points to 19.4% in Q2, driven by favorable mix, cost containment, and improved absorption despite higher R&D investment.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. operating margin ~18.5% (est.)\u201d; the Q1 roadmap said \u201cSecond-half weighted margin expansion \u2014 Q2 is not the margin quarter.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 free cash flow was substantially above the preview\u2019s $120-$135 million estimate, and full-year free cash flow guidance also moved higher. The quarterly result was a much stronger cash outcome than expected.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFree cash flow reached $160 million, a 37% year-over-year improvement, representing a strong 116% cash conversion rate\u201d; \u201cFree cash flow guidance was raised to a record $585-$605 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cFree cash flow: ~$120\u2013135M (est.)\u201d and FY26 guidance of \u201c$580\u2013600M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Defense and Electronics orders were far stronger than the preview\u2019s discussion of a deliberately flat Q2 sales-and-margin bar: Q2 orders nearly doubled year over year and year-to-date orders were up more than 30%. The preview did not anticipate a record order performance of this scale.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDefense and Electronics orders surged nearly 50% year-over-year in Q2 and are up over 30% year-to-date, marking a record segment performance.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cDefense Electronics: sales and margins expected to be roughly flat vs. prior-year Q2.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management described the year as heavily back-loaded, with Q3 operating income and margin flat sequentially and a record Q4 expected to deliver margin above 20%. That is a more concentrated and timing-dependent second-half profile than the preview\u2019s general expectation of second-half-weighted margin expansion.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cManagement expects a heavily back-loaded year, with Q3 operating income and margin flat sequentially and a record Q4 delivering operating margin in excess of 20%, driven by Defense Electronics timing.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cSecond-half weighted margin expansion \u2014 Q2 is not the margin quarter.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 diluted EPS growth of 15% was above the roughly 12.1% growth implied by the preview\u2019s consensus estimate, and management said the result exceeded its own expectations. The call facts do not give an exact EPS figure, so the size of any beat against the stated per-share consensus cannot be pinned down precisely.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDiluted EPS up 15%, exceeding company expectations.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. diluted EPS: $3.61\u2013$3.62\u201d versus Q2 FY25 adjusted EPS of \u201c$3.23,\u201d implying \u201c+12.1%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Curtiss-Wright reported approximately $95 million in Maritime Industrial Base funding, up from $70 million at the end of March, supporting its Pennsylvania facility expansion. The preview noted that the expansion was backed by maritime industrial base funding but did not anticipate this larger award amount.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cCurtiss-Wright has now been awarded approximately $95 million in Maritime Industrial Base funding, up from $70 million at the end of March, supporting an $80 million multiyear Pennsylvania facility expansion.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described the \u201c$80M Cheswick, PA expansion\u201d as \u201cbacked by the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania and maritime industrial base funding,\u201d without specifying an award amount.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 sales of $924 million were marginally below the preview\u2019s $926.2 million consensus estimate. The gap was small, and the reported 5% growth remained consistent with management\u2019s mid-single-digit Q2 outlook.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 2026 sales of $924 million grew 5% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cRevenue: $926.2M\u201d; management\u2019s Q2 sales roadmap was \u201cmid-single-digit.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Q2 sales growth of 5% was consistent with management\u2019s \u201cmid-single-digit\u201d Q2 roadmap, despite the slight shortfall against consensus.", "Q2 book-to-bill above 1.1x was above the preview\u2019s sub-1.0x concern threshold and within its base-case expectation of roughly 1.0-1.2x.", "Management continued to expect the AP1000 reactor coolant pump order in 2026, as the preview said it was still expected this year and explicitly not included in guidance."]}
{"event_id": "ea_OFIX_Q2_2026", "ticker": "OFIX", "event_datetime": "2026-08-05T12:30:00+00:00", "car1": -0.14282932171388563, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Orthofix reported 5% pro forma constant-currency sales growth, below the roughly 6% Q2 growth management had said was needed. U.S. Spine Fixation grew only 3%, with steep declines among smaller distributors despite continued strength from the top 30.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201c5% pro forma constant currency net sales growth\u201d in Q2; \u201cU.S. Spine Fixation grew only 3% due to steep declines among smaller distributors,\u201d while the top 30 distributors \u201ccontinued performing well.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cQ2 needed ~6% cc growth to hit the ~5% H1 target\u201d; Q1 had called the first wave of distributor transition \u201clargely behind us,\u201d and the preview flagged any repeat of disruption as a fresh negative.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The company now expects core organic growth in the second half to slow to roughly 2\u20133%, with Q3 sales roughly flat against Q2. That is weaker than the preview\u2019s expectation for approximately 6% H2 growth and acceleration.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cExcluding the ~$15 million EU MDR benefit, core organic revenue growth in the second half is expected to slow to roughly 2-3%,\u201d and \u201cQ3 net sales are expected to be roughly consistent with Q2.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said management had flagged \u201c~6% in H2\u201d and described the expected trajectory as accelerating to that level.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Orthofix disclosed a European distributor arrangement that is expected to add about $15 million of 2026 sales, mostly in Q4, but create an approximately $22 million sales headwind in 2027. The preview did not anticipate this sizable timing effect.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The arrangement is \u201cexpected to contribute ~$15 million of incremental net sales in 2026 (mostly in Q4), but creates an approximately $22 million net sales headwind in 2027.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Global Limb Reconstruction grew 11%, above the preview\u2019s roughly 7% estimate, but the growth was led by international demand while U.S. growth came in below expectations.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Global Limb Reconstruction grew \u201c11% on a constant currency basis to $37.7 million,\u201d led by international demand for TrueLok Elevate and FITBONE; \u201cU.S. growth came in below expectations.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated Global Limb Reconstruction at \u201c~$35.5M (+7% rep.)\u201d and listed a return to double-digit U.S. growth in H2 as a product/operating item to watch.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Biologics reached roughly flat year-over-year sales for a second consecutive quarter, improving from the double-digit declines in 2025 and avoiding the renewed disruption the preview identified as a risk. Management nevertheless stopped short of saying the return to growth was sustained.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cBiologics net sales were approximately flat year-over-year,\u201d marking \u201ca second consecutive quarter of improvement from double-digit declines in 2025,\u201d though management did not declare a sustained return to growth.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview warned that \u201cany repeat of the biologics disruption is a fresh negative.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["FY2026 net sales guidance of $845\u2013855 million matched the preview\u2019s base-case range of roughly $845\u2013855 million, although its midpoint is above the cited $843.3 million consensus.", "Adjusted EBITDA guidance of $95\u201398 million matched the preview\u2019s anticipated full restoration to $95\u201398 million.", "Q2 adjusted EBITDA of $20.1 million was within the preview\u2019s modeled range of approximately $19\u201321 million.", "CMS\u2019s restoration of the prior Medicare reimbursement level was already known before the release; the preview described the reversal and identified reinstatement of guidance as the key question."]}
{"event_id": "ea_RKLB_Q2_2026", "ticker": "RKLB", "event_datetime": "2026-08-10T21:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.0021931196967351907, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "The preview expected a modest Q2 revenue beat, with its base case at $238\u2013246 million and a history of beating the top of guidance. Rocket Lab reported $234 million: within its $225\u2013240 million guide and slightly above the roughly $232 million consensus, but short of the preview\u2019s expected beat.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cRecord Q2 2026 revenue of $234 million \u2026 landing within prior guidance.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cBase \u2026 Revenue $238\u2013246M\u201d; \u201cRKLB has beaten the top of its own guide repeatedly.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview expected Q2 gross margins of 33\u201335% GAAP and 38\u201340% non-GAAP. Actual margins were higher at 36.1% and 41.5%, respectively, though a nonrecurring tariff refund helped and an inventory reserve partly offset it.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 non-GAAP gross margin of 41.5% and GAAP gross margin of 36.1% both exceeded guidance, aided by a nonrecurring tariff refund offset by an inventory reserve.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cGAAP gross margin 33\u201335%\u201d; \u201cNon-GAAP gross margin 38\u201340%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The Q3 revenue guide was stronger than the preview\u2019s base-case range: $250\u2013265 million, with a $257.5 million midpoint, compared with an expected $250\u2013260 million and a roughly $245 million level implied by FY26 consensus.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ3 2026 guidance calls for revenue of $250\u2013265 million (10% sequential growth at midpoint).\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cBase \u2026 Q3 guide $250\u2013260M\u201d; \u201cQ3 revenue guide vs. ~$245M implied by FY26 consensus\u201d; \u201cA Q3 guide of $255M+ is a meaningful beat.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview focused on the Q2 margin step-down but did not give a Q3 margin forecast. Rocket Lab now guides to Q3 gross margins below its Q2 results\u201429\u201331% GAAP and 35\u201337% non-GAAP\u2014citing unfavorable Space Systems mix.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ3 2026 guidance calls for \u2026 lower gross margins (GAAP 29\u201331%, non-GAAP 35\u201337%) due to unfavorable Space Systems mix.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "The preview expected Q2 backlog to be roughly $2.2 billion and noted that major recent awards would land in Q3. Instead, backlog ended Q2 at $2.36 billion, while the company also reported over $1 billion in new contracts across Q2 and post-quarter.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cRocket Lab ended Q2 with $2.36 billion total backlog\u201d; \u201csigned over $1 billion in new contracts across launch and Space Systems during Q2 and post-quarter.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cBacklog \u2026 ~$2.2B\u201d; \u201cQ2 ended before both the $266M RSLP \u2026 and the $397M SB-AMTI Flatellite award \u2026 Those land in Q3 backlog.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Rocket Lab kept Neutron\u2019s Q4 2026 pad-delivery plan, as the preview hoped, but management said the year-end launch window is narrowing. That makes the timing less secure than the preview\u2019s expected \u201cQ4, tracking\u201d language, even as the company prioritizes scaling to Flight 10.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNeutron production remains on track for delivery to the pad in Q4 2026, though management acknowledged the year-end launch window is narrowing while prioritizing scaling to Flight 10.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cWatch for \u2026 whether Beck says \u2018Q4\u2019 or hedges to \u2018around year-end\u2019\u201d; base case: \u201cNeutron \u2018Q4, tracking.\u2019\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The call disclosed Neutron pricing of $50\u201355 million per launch, with no significant early discounting and management seeing more upside than downside to pricing. The preview discussed Electron ASP but did not anticipate this pricing information for Neutron.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNeutron is being sold at a $50-55 million ASP with no significant early discounting, and management sees more upside than downside to launch pricing given a supply-constrained market.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "The call disclosed a pending Iridium acquisition expected to close in mid-2027, adding a 66-satellite constellation, 2.5 million subscribers, about $870 million in annual revenue, and significant free cash flow. The preview did not flag this acquisition.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe pending Iridium acquisition, expected to close mid-2027, adds a 66-satellite constellation with 2.5 million subscribers and roughly $870 million in annual revenue, providing significant free cash flow.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Free cash flow use worsened to $110.1 million from $77.4 million in Q1, due to Neutron tail production and Mynaric supply-chain replenishment. The preview did not provide a free-cash-flow expectation; management said cash-flow positivity is expected only 18\u201324 months after Neutron\u2019s first successful launch.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFree cash flow deteriorated to a use of $110.1 million in Q2 (from $77.4 million in Q1), driven by Neutron tail production and Mynaric supply chain replenishment, with cash flow positivity expected 18-24 months after Neutron's first successful launch.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "Rocket Lab raised $1.08 billion through its now-terminated ATM equity program and ended Q2 with roughly $2.4 billion in cash and equivalents. That is substantially more cash than the preview\u2019s Q1 baseline, improving funding capacity but reflecting a large equity raise.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company raised $1.08 billion through its now-terminated at-the-market equity program, ending Q2 with roughly $2.4 billion in cash and equivalents to fund M&A and growth initiatives.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cQ1 FY26 baseline \u2026 cash $1.48B (~$2.0B total liquidity).\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Q2 revenue was within the company\u2019s $225\u2013240 million guidance range and close to the roughly $232 million consensus estimate."]}
{"event_id": "ea_FA_Q2_2026", "ticker": "FA", "event_datetime": "2026-08-06T12:30:00+00:00", "car1": 0.17467947387543614, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "FA reported Q2 revenue of $449 million, $34 million above the preview\u2019s $415 million consensus and 15% year-over-year growth versus the 6.3% implied growth. Even excluding the episodic customer initiatives, management estimated growth of 11%\u201312%, still well above the expected pace.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 2026 revenue grew 15% year-over-year to $449 million\u201d; \u201cExcluding the one-time customer initiative benefits, management estimated Q2 total revenue growth was still 11% to 12%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cRevenue | $415.0M\u201d; \u201cImplied Y/Y | +6.3%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted diluted EPS was $0.35, well above the preview\u2019s $0.29 consensus\u2014about 21% higher than the expected level, rather than the modest 5.6% year-over-year growth implied by consensus.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAdjusted diluted EPS rose 30% year-over-year to $0.35.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. diluted EPS | $0.29\u201d; \u201cImplied Y/Y | +5.6%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Base revenue grew 6.7%, far stronger than the preview\u2019s 0% to -2% assumption. The quality is partly tempered by episodic customer initiatives: roughly half the base growth came from them, leaving underlying base growth at about 3.3%, and management expects the initiatives to normalize by Q4.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cBase revenue grew an exceptionally strong 6.7%, with roughly half attributed to episodic customer-driven volume initiatives expected to normalize by Q4, implying underlying base growth of about 3.3%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cManagement's FY26 assumption is 0% to -2%\u201d; the preview also said base revenue \u201cturned flat in Q1.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "FA raised its full-year 2026 guidance across revenue, EBITDA and EPS, rather than merely reaffirming or narrowly lifting the range. The revenue floor rose by $45 million to $1.67 billion, and the EPS range moved to $1.23\u2013$1.29 from $1.15\u2013$1.25.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company raised full-year 2026 guidance across all metrics, now expecting revenue of $1.67-$1.71 billion, adjusted EBITDA of $472-$486 million, and adjusted diluted EPS of $1.23-$1.29, with the bottom end raised by $45 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described a \u201cnarrow to ~$1,650\u2013$1,700M\u201d as the \u201crealistic good outcome,\u201d and said \u201ca flat reaffirm is a modest negative\u201d; prior revenue guidance was \u201c$1,625M \u2013 $1,700M\u201d and EPS guidance \u201c$1.15 \u2013 $1.25.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted EBITDA was $128.5 million versus the preview\u2019s roughly $116 million estimate, and the 28.6% margin topped the roughly 28.0% expected level. The margin beat the preview\u2019s concern about a weak year-over-year comparison, although it remained below Q2 2025\u2019s 29.2% margin.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAdjusted EBITDA increased 13% to $128.5 million with a margin of 28.6%, up 130 basis points sequentially.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. EBITDA | ~$116M (derived @ 28% mgmt-framed margin)\u201d; \u201cAdj. EBITDA margin | ~28.0%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "FA signed 20 enterprise bookings with at least $500,000 in expected annual contract value, up from 17 in Q1. That reached the preview\u2019s 20-plus-booking bull-case marker rather than merely matching the prior-quarter level.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201c20 enterprise bookings (each with expected annual contract value of at least $500,000) signed in Q2, up from 17 in Q1.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview reported \u201cQ1 delivered 17 enterprise bookings\u201d and set \u201c20+ enterprise bookings\u201d as part of its bull scenario.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Debt reduction was ahead of the preview\u2019s stated trajectory: cumulative voluntary debt prepayments since the Sterling acquisition reached over $165.5 million, versus $120.5 million noted in the preview, and net leverage fell to 3.7x from the preview\u2019s 3.9x.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company made a $45 million voluntary debt prepayment this week on top of a $25 million prepayment in Q2, bringing cumulative debt repayment since the Sterling acquisition to over $165.5 million and reducing net leverage to 3.7x.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201c$120.5M cumulative voluntary debt prepayment since the Sterling close\u201d; \u201cSynergized net leverage improved 4.4x \u2192 3.9x.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Customer retention was 96%, a little below the preview\u2019s 97% repeat assumption.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cCustomer retention held at 96%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview\u2019s base-case scenario expected \u201c97% retention repeats.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Combined upsell, cross-sell and new-logo revenue grew 12.5%, broadly in line with the preview\u2019s statement that this growth was already running at about 12%.", "Management expected growth to moderate in the second half, guiding to mid-to-high single-digit growth in Q3 and low-to-mid single-digit growth in Q4 as it laps strong 2025 comparisons. This was consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that H2 caution and moderation would remain."]}
{"event_id": "ea_JILL_Q2_2026", "ticker": "JILL", "event_datetime": "2026-09-09T12:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.10116867140513941, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "J.Jill reported $154.8 million in sales, up 0.5%, and comps up 0.5%, versus expectations for sales down 1% to 3% and comps down 2% to 4%. Both measures turned positive instead of declining.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 total company sales rose 0.5% year-over-year to $154.8 million with comparable sales also up 0.5%, exceeding company guidance.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Company guidance was \u201c-1% to -3%\u201d for sales and \u201c-2% to -4%\u201d for comparable sales; working revenue consensus was \u201c~$150\u2013152M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The company received $13.3 million in net tariff refunds, a substantial benefit that the preview said was not included in guidance. Those refunds helped lift reported adjusted EBITDA to $32.8 million, well above the expected $18\u201320 million range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company received $13.3 million in net tariff refunds in the quarter\u201d; reported adjusted EBITDA was \u201c$32.8 million\u201d versus $25.6 million a year earlier, \u201cboosted by $13.3 million in net tariff refunds.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said J.Jill had \u201cassumed zero refund benefit in guidance\u201d and that any recognized refund was \u201cpure upside\u201d; Q2 adjusted EBITDA guidance was \u201c$18\u201320M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted diluted EPS was $1.24, far above the $0.57 consensus estimate. The preview\u2019s bridge said consensus was effectively assuming adjusted EBITDA at the top of the company\u2019s range, while the reported EPS was more than double the estimate.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAdjusted diluted EPS jumped to $1.24 from $0.81 a year earlier, aided by roughly $0.01 from share repurchases.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. diluted EPS\u201d consensus was \u201c$0.57\u201d; the preview described this as \u201ceffectively underwriting the top of management's own EBITDA range.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Excluding tariff refunds, gross margin was roughly flat at 68.3%, better than the expected decline of about 100 basis points. Reported gross margin reached 76.8%, with the much larger increase reflecting the tariff-refund benefit.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cReported gross margin expanded about 840 basis points to 76.8%, but excluding tariff refunds gross margin was roughly flat at 68.3%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The Q2 company guide was for gross margin down \u201c~-100 bps\u201d to about 67.4%.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "J.Jill raised its full-year outlook: adjusted EBITDA guidance increased to $75\u201380 million from the preview\u2019s $70\u201375 million, and the sales outlook improved from flat to down 2% to flat to up 2%. The new outlook also calls for comps from down 1% to up 1% and gross margin up 100\u2013150 basis points year over year.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cManagement raised full-year guidance to adjusted EBITDA of $75-80 million, sales flat to up 2%, comps down 1% to up 1%, and gross margin up 100-150 basis points versus prior year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described the full-year adjusted EBITDA guide as \u201c$70\u201375M\u201d and the sales guide as \u201cflat-to-down-2%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The Q3 outlook calls for sales up 3\u20135%, comps up 1\u20133%, flat gross margins, and adjusted EBITDA of $20\u201322 million. That is a stronger near-term outlook than the preview\u2019s expectation of gradual sequential improvement through Q3, particularly with positive Q3 comps rather than continued declines.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ3 guidance calls for sales up 3-5%, comps up 1-3%, flat gross margins, and adjusted EBITDA of $20-22 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described the expected pattern as \u201cgradual sequential improvement through Q3, accelerating into Q4,\u201d and its base scenario assumed Q2 comps of \u201c-2.5% to -3.5%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview expected any tariff refunds to be pure upside to EBITDA, but J.Jill said it intentionally put most of the $13.3 million refund into marketing and technology rather than letting the amount flow to the bottom line. That prioritizes investment over near-term earnings benefit.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company intentionally chose to invest most of it into marketing and technology initiatives rather than dropping it to the bottom line.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview called any recognized refund \u201cpure upside to the print and to FY EBITDA.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The company\u2019s full-year net new store plan is now 1\u20133 openings, below the preview\u2019s cited range of 1\u20135. Two openings have been pushed into early 2027 because of landlord delivery delays.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFull-year net new store openings were trimmed to 1-3 due to landlord delivery delays pushing two openings into early 2027.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview cited the net new store plan as cut \u201cto 1\u20135 from ~5.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Inventory ended the quarter down about 5% year over year, a favorable development against the preview\u2019s concern about inventory and back-half markdown risk. The preview had reported Q1 inventory up 5.4% as reported and identified promotional Q3/Q4 margin as a risk if inventory rose against weak comps.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cInventory ended the quarter down about 5% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview flagged \u201cInventory and back-half markdown risk,\u201d noting Q1 inventory was \u201c+5.4% reported\u201d and that rising inventory against negative comps could mean \u201cpromotional Q3/Q4 margin.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The full-year free-cash-flow expectation increased to approximately $40 million, double the $20 million target cited in the preview.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company expects full-year free cash flow of approximately $40 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview cited a \u201c$20M FY target\u201d for free cash flow.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management reported favorable customer-cohort signals not anticipated in the preview: new-to-brand customers are younger, retain at higher rates, and spend more through higher order values and more trips; lapsed-customer reactivation is also building.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNew-to-brand customers are trending younger, retaining at higher rates, and spending more via higher average order value and more trips, while lapsed-customer reactivation is building.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview noted that retail was \u201cwhere new-to-brand customer growth is coming from,\u201d but did not anticipate the stronger retention and spending trends or building lapsed-customer reactivation.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Underlying adjusted EBITDA, excluding tariff refunds and strategic investments, was $20.1 million\u2014only slightly above the $18\u201320 million guidance range. The underlying result was therefore near the top of the expected range, rather than matching the much larger reported EBITDA increase.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cUnderlying adjusted EBITDA (excluding tariff refunds and strategic investments) was $20.1 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Q2 adjusted EBITDA guidance was \u201c$18\u201320M,\u201d with consensus around \u201c$19\u201320M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": []}
{"event_id": "ea_GCMG_Q2_2026", "ticker": "GCMG", "event_datetime": "2026-08-10T14:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.01806594503338388, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "The firm\u2019s roughly $150 million SpaceX investment, bought at an average of $6.37 per share, was valued at about $3.5 billion\u2014its largest single-issuer gain ever. The ARS composite returned about 14% gross for the quarter including SpaceX and 10% excluding it, but the gain remains unrealized and subject to lockup.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe firm's ~$150 million investment in SpaceX (average cost of $6.37 per share) was valued at approximately $3.5 billion as of last week, representing the largest single-issuer gain in the firm's history, though gains remain unrealized and subject to lockup.\u201d The ARS composite delivered \u201c~14% gross returns for the quarter including SpaceX and ~10% excluding it.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "AUM ended the quarter at $97 billion and fee-paying AUM at $78 billion, both about 13% higher year over year\u2014above the preview\u2019s respective $93\u201395 billion and $75\u201377 billion estimates.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cGCM Grosvenor ended Q2 2026 with $97 billion in AUM and $78 billion in fee-paying AUM, both up approximately 13% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAUM \u2026 $93\u201395B\u201d and \u201cFee-paying AUM \u2026 $75\u201377B.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "FRE reached $50 million, above the preview\u2019s $48\u201349 million estimate. The 45% FRE margin, however, was within the expected 44\u201345% range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFee-related earnings grew 21% to $50 million (45% FRE margin).\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cFee-related earnings (FRE) \u2026 $48\u201349M\u201d and \u201cFRE margin \u2026 44\u201345%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted net income growth of 22% implies roughly $39 million using the preview\u2019s cited $32.1 million year-ago figure, at or just above the top of the $36\u201339 million estimate range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAdjusted net income grew 22% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cAdj. net income / share \u2026 $36\u201339M / ~$0.18\u20130.19.\u201d The preview also cited Q2 2025 ANI of \u201c$32.1M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview highlighted wealth fundraising, but the reported year-to-date contribution from individual investors and insurance channels was notably concentrated: they provided 23% and 18% of fundraising despite representing only 5% and 4% of AUM at the start of the year.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cIndividual investor and insurance channels drove 23% and 18% of year-to-date fundraising despite representing only 5% and 4% of AUM at the start of the year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cWealth channel + evergreen products \u2014 Q1 wealth raise was ~$500M (historically elevated).\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Unrealized carried interest was $965 million gross and $493 million for the firm, slightly below the preview\u2019s cited Q1 levels of $1.0 billion and $510 million. Management pointed to a potential increase next quarter as the lagged SpaceX mark rises, so the expected mark-up has not yet appeared in these figures.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cGross unrealized carried interest was $965 million ($493 million firm share) as of June 30, with a meaningful increase expected next quarter as the one-quarter-lagged SpaceX mark rises from the current $84 per share.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cWatch unrealized carry (Q1: $1.0B gross / $510M firm share, +23% YoY) \u2014 growth here without realization is a \u2018later, not never\u2019 signal.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Q2 fundraising was $2.3 billion, within the preview\u2019s $2.0\u20132.5 billion range and above the prior-quarter $1.5 billion level; management\u2019s guidance that second-half fundraising would exceed first-half fundraising was also consistent with its previously stated back-half-over-front-half bar.", "Fee-related revenue of $111 million was at the top of the preview\u2019s $109\u2013111 million range.", "The credit platform and inaugural credit secondaries fund were broadly in line with the preview\u2019s framing: it described a $17 billion-plus credit franchise and anticipated the approximately $1.2 billion secondaries fund\u2019s final close as a Q3 event. The call reported nearly $18 billion in credit AUM and a fund raising about $1.2 billion.", "The estimated $35\u201340 million in unrealized annual performance fees was close to the preview\u2019s cited roughly $35 million run-rate; management also said most fees crystallize in Q4, consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that ARS performance fees predominantly crystallize then."]}
{"event_id": "ea_INKT_Q2_2026", "ticker": "INKT", "event_datetime": "2026-08-13T12:30:00+00:00", "car1": -0.038158988237889324, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "The call reported encouraging outcomes for the first two run-in patients, but did not provide the larger patient update or any control-arm information the preview said investors should look for. The clinical observations were positive, but the dataset itself was not a meaningful expansion beyond the previously disclosed anecdotes.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cInitial run-in data on the first two treated patients showed they were alive and fever-free at day 28 with improved oxygenation and ARDS resolution.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cThe bar for today: more patients, and any hint of the control arm. Uncontrolled n-of-few anecdotes at Day-28 have already been paid for.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The named-patient program was confirmed to be paid, with initial-patient revenue expected in the Q3 update. The preview identified whether the program was cost-recovery or free-of-charge as an open question, so this adds a concrete early commercial signal, though it does not establish revenue in Q2.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cMiNK launched its first paid named-patient access program for agent 797 in Brazil, with revenue from initial patients to be reported in the Q3 update.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cKey questions: which jurisdictions, is it cost-recovery or free-of-charge, is revenue recognized, what per-patient economics\u2026\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management said it plans to seek an FDA meeting in the coming weeks to convert the randomized phase 2 into a seamless phase 3, and identified 28-day mortality as the FDA-driven primary endpoint. The preview noted that a seamless phase 3 pathway was designed, but did not anticipate this near-term FDA step or the specified primary endpoint.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cManagement plans to seek an FDA meeting in the coming weeks to convert the randomized phase 2 into a seamless phase 3 study, with 28-day mortality as the FDA-driven primary endpoint.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cA randomized 1:1 Phase 2 \u2026 with a seamless Phase 3 pathway designed.\u201d The preview listed \u201coverall survival, ventilator-free days, ICU days\u201d as primary endpoints.", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Cash ended the quarter at $8.8 million, within the preview\u2019s $7.0\u2013$9.5 million estimate range and above its roughly $7.8 million point estimate.", "Net loss of $3.1 million and EPS of negative $0.62 were close to the preview\u2019s roughly $2.9 million loss and negative $0.59 estimate, and within its stated ranges.", "Operating cash use rose to $2.1 million, consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that burn would step up as the randomized ARDS study launched.", "The first patient was dosed in the randomized ARDS study, consistent with the preview\u2019s description of the trial as enrolling. The preliminary randomized readout timing of the first half of 2027 was also broadly consistent with the preview\u2019s early-2027 timing expectation.", "The 77% disease control rate in PD-1-refractory gastroesophageal cancer was already reported in the preview, as were the absence of major agent-797-attributed serious adverse events in initial patients."]}
{"event_id": "ea_ESAB_Q2_2026", "ticker": "ESAB", "event_datetime": "2026-08-06T12:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.02358893194344358, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Q2 sales of $766 million exceeded the preview\u2019s $747\u2013750 million consensus range, while core organic growth was positive at 2.5% rather than the preview\u2019s base-case flat to \u22121%.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cRecord Q2 total sales of $766 million, up 13% year-over-year, with core organic growth of 2.5%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cRevenue: $747.3M \u2013 $750.1M\u201d; the base scenario expected \u201cOrganic ~flat to \u22121%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The full-year adjusted EPS range of $5.40\u2013$5.50 is below the preview\u2019s cited $5.70\u2013$5.90 guidance, despite the higher sales and EBITDA outlook. The preview had flagged the EPS bridge as vulnerable, but the reported range is a concrete reduction from the prior guide.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFull-year adjusted EPS guidance is $5.40\u2013$5.50.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201ccore adj. EPS $5.70\u20135.90\u201d; the preview called the EPS ramp \u201cthe single most vulnerable number in the model.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management\u2019s full-year EPS outlook still incorporates a $15 million drag from price-cost neutrality, logistics, and commodity inflation, rather than the price-cost improvement later in the year the preview said management had guided toward.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFull-year adjusted EPS guidance is $5.40\u2013$5.50, incorporating an assumed $15 million drag from transitory price-cost neutrality from logistics and commodity inflation.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Management had \u201cguided to price-cost neutral in Q2 and price-cost positive later in the year.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The Middle East is weaker and more costly than the preview\u2019s framing quantified: the region is down roughly 10\u201311%, and logistics costs have tripled. Management said rebuild activity could eventually restore prior growth, but expects that recovery to come after the current downturn.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe Middle East, representing 7\u20138% of ESAB's business, was down double digits (~10\u201311%) with logistics costs tripling due to conflict, though rebuild efforts could restore prior ~20% growth rates.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described the Middle East as \u201c\u2248 7% of sales,\u201d cited Iran-related costs and rerouted inventory, and said to watch whether the drag was \u201cpersisting\u201d rather than \u201ceasing.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management disclosed an approximately $450 million DFI cross-selling funnel with ESAB, a sizeable opportunity not quantified in the preview, but said conversion will take time and materialize mainly in 2027 and beyond.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDFI's cross-selling funnel with ESAB is approaching $450 million, though management expects conversion to take time and materialize mainly in 2027 and beyond.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["Q2 adjusted EBITDA of $150 million was at the top of the preview\u2019s implied $146\u2013150 million range; the 19.5% margin was at the bottom of the preview\u2019s 19.5\u201320.0% range and the preview had anticipated a decline of up to 90 basis points.", "Q2 price-cost neutrality was consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that neutrality would occur in Q2.", "The 2\u20134% full-year organic growth range remained consistent with the prior guidance cited in the preview.", "DFI\u2019s high-single-digit growth, approximately 65% gross margins, and roughly 30% EBITDA margins were consistent with the deal economics described in the preview."]}
{"event_id": "ea_CEVA_Q2_2026", "ticker": "CEVA", "event_datetime": "2026-08-10T12:30:00+00:00", "car1": -0.17416102133320954, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Q2 revenue was $29 million, above the preview\u2019s $28.15 million consensus and $28.6 million base case, though still within the company\u2019s $26\u201330 million guidance range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "Q2 2026 revenue rose 13% year-over-year and 7% sequentially to $29 million, exceeding internal expectations.", "expectation_evidence": "Consensus Q2'26 revenue: $28.15M (Investing.com) and $28.6M (ChartMill/Zacks-style); our base: $28.6M.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Royalties reached $10.8 million, well above the preview\u2019s roughly $10 million estimate and slightly higher year-over-year rather than down 7% as forecast. The sequential increase was also strong, although the preview had emphasized the risk to the H2 recovery.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Royalty revenue was $10.8 million, up 17% sequentially but only marginally above the prior-year's $10.7 million.", "expectation_evidence": "Royalty base: $10.0M (\u20137% YoY); the preview said royalties at or above $10M would support the FY bridge.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "CEVA raised its full-year revenue growth outlook to 13\u201315%, exceeding the preview\u2019s expectation that it would reaffirm the top end of its prior 8\u201312% range.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company raised its full-year 2026 revenue growth outlook to 13-15%, up from the previous 12% guidance.", "expectation_evidence": "Management guided FY26 revenue to the top end of +8\u201312%; the preview expected the \u201ctop end of 8\u201312%\u201d to be reaffirmed.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The 2026 operating-income growth outlook is now approximately 70%, above the preview\u2019s expected +40\u201350% range. The net-income outlook of approximately 50% is at the top of that expected range, rather than an additional surprise.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management now expects non-GAAP operating income to grow approximately 70% and non-GAAP net income approximately 50% year-over-year for 2026.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview cited a +40\u201350% YoY target for non-GAAP operating income/net income and flagged whether it would be reiterated or trimmed.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The preview already highlighted the platform-company AI-silicon win, but the reported characterization adds a favorable economic detail: this customer category is expected to produce meaningfully higher royalty per unit than typical NPU offerings.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "A leading global AI and computing platform company selected CEVA's new NPU IP for its next-generation custom AI silicon, a new category of customer expected to yield meaningfully higher royalty per unit than typical NPU offerings.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview described the July 6 win with a major U.S. software/AI platform company and said to watch for royalty economics; it did not state that this customer category would yield higher royalty per unit.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management flagged memory pricing and broader supply constraints as risks to second-half handset royalties, tempering the H2 recovery narrative the preview said was needed to support the full-year outlook.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management flagged memory pricing dynamics and broader supply constraints as key risk variables that could affect second-half handset royalties.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said an unqualified H2 recovery would support the FY bridge and listed memory-shortage commentary turning to \u201cnormalizing\u201d as a bull-case signal.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "CEVA signed 10 licensing agreements, below the preview\u2019s expectation that deal count would hold in the low-to-mid teens. The quarter did include two first-time customers and two direct OEM deals.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "CEVA signed 10 licensing agreements in the quarter, including two with first-time customers and two directly with OEMs.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said to watch for licensing agreement count \u201cholding in the low-to-mid teens.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Non-GAAP EPS was 8 cents, at the top of the preview\u2019s $0.073\u2013$0.08 consensus range, though below its $0.09 base-case estimate.", "Licensing and related revenue of $18.2 million was close to the preview\u2019s roughly $18 million implied estimate, despite coming in below its $18.6 million base case."]}
{"event_id": "ea_INFU_Q2_2026", "ticker": "INFU", "event_datetime": "2026-08-04T13:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.3175545072932473, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Wound Care growth was much stronger than the preview\u2019s roughly $2.5 million base estimate and $2.4 million watch threshold: revenue rose 154%, an increase of $2.1 million. That growth rate was also well above the preview\u2019s roughly 80% benchmark.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cWound Care revenue surged 154% ($2.1 million), driven mainly by compression devices for lymphedema.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cBase (50%): \u2026 wound care ~$2.5M\u201d; the catalyst checklist called for \u201cWound care revenue \u2265 $2.4M and growth rate \u2265 ~80% Y/Y.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Diluted EPS was $0.15, above the preview\u2019s $0.13 estimate and the cited $0.105 consensus. The per-share result was a clearer upside surprise than the preview\u2019s already-expected EPS beat.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNet income increased to $3.2 million ($0.15 per diluted share) from $2.6 million ($0.12 per diluted share) a year earlier.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cMy Q2 FY26E\u201d diluted EPS was \u201c$0.13\u201d; consensus diluted EPS was \u201c$0.105.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Device Solutions revenue fell 16.1%, a steeper reported decline than the preview\u2019s estimate of a 9% decline. The segment\u2019s margin improvement was strong, but its reported revenue trajectory was weaker than modeled.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cDevice Solutions margin rising to 50.2% from 41.9% despite a 16.1% revenue decline in that segment.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated Device Solutions at \u201c\u22129% Y/Y reported; ~+4% ex-GE.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Patient Services gross margin fell to 61.8%, below the preview\u2019s Q1 reference of 64.8% and the key question of whether the margin pressure would be contained. Management attributed the decline to lower-margin Wound Care mix and higher pump maintenance costs.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cPatient Services gross margin declined to 61.8% from 64.2% due to a larger mix of lower-margin Wound Care revenue and higher pump maintenance costs.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview identified \u201cPatient Services gross margin (64.8% in Q1)\u201d as a key metric and said whether wage and benefit pressure was contained would determine the margin story.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Adjusted EBITDA and margin modestly exceeded the preview\u2019s estimates: approximately $8.6 million and 23.4%, versus $8.4 million and 23.1%.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cAdjusted EBITDA rose 7.6% to approximately $8.6 million, with EBITDA margin expanding over 1 point to 23.4% from 22.3% a year ago.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated \u201cAdj. EBITDA $8.4M (23.1% margin).\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Consolidated gross margin came in slightly above the preview\u2019s estimate, at 58% versus approximately 57.5%.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cConsolidated gross margin expanded to 58% from 55.2%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated \u201cGross margin ~57.5% (+230bps Y/Y).\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Revenue of $36.9 million was modestly above the preview\u2019s $36.35 million working consensus, though below the cited $37.08 million alternate estimate. This was a small headline beat rather than a clear beat across the full range of estimates.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 2026 revenue reached a record $36.9 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview cited Q2 revenue consensus of \u201c$36.35M,\u201d with an alternate estimate of \u201c$37.08M,\u201d and used \u201c$36.4M\u201d as its working estimate.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The company reported $4.4 million of buybacks, a sizable deployment against the preview\u2019s expectation that only about one month of the new authorization would fall in the quarter and that post-quarter commentary would be the main signal.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The company ended the quarter \u201cwhile returning $4.4 million to shareholders via buybacks.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview noted the new authorization opened July 1 and that \u201conly ~1 month of the new program falls inside this reporting quarter,\u201d with the disclosure to watch being \u201cpost-quarter commentary, not the Q2 cash flow statement.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["Pro forma revenue growth was 7.5%, within the company\u2019s 6%\u20138% annual growth guidance and close to the preview\u2019s roughly 7% base-case expectation.", "Management reaffirmed the 6%\u20138% pro forma annual revenue growth guidance and low-to-mid-20% adjusted EBITDA margin guidance, as the preview expected."]}
{"event_id": "ea_VNDA_Q2_2026", "ticker": "VNDA", "event_datetime": "2026-08-05T20:30:00+00:00", "car1": 0.00643931425827271, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Vanda reported a $62.5 million Q2 net loss, substantially worse than the preview's modeled loss of about $46 million and its translation of consensus EPS into a loss near $36 million. Higher R&D and SG&A tied to clinical programs and commercial launches drove the result.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cVanda posted a Q2 2026 net loss of $62.5 million,\u201d driven by \u201chigher R&D and SG&A tied to clinical programs and commercial launches.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview modeled diluted EPS of \u201c\u2248 \u2212$0.76\u201d and said consensus EPS of \u201c\u2212$0.60 implies a net loss near $36M.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Hetlios sales were far below the preview's $14.5 million estimate at $5.6 million. A roughly $7 million shipment was pushed into Q3, which explains most of the gap, but generic competition also contributed to the decline.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cHetlios net product sales fell 66% year-over-year to $5.6 million\u201d due to generic competition and \u201ca shipment timing issue that pushed roughly $7 million of revenue into Q3 2026.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated \u201cHETLIOZ\u201d at \u201c$14.5M\u201d for Q2 and flagged generic erosion.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Nereus recognized only $1 million of sales, below the preview's $2.5 million estimate and at the preview's warned-about $0\u20131 million downside level. Most initial wholesaler stocking was constrained, so the launch's first sales datapoint was weaker than the central expectation.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNereus generated only $1 million in recognized net product sales\u201d; \u201c$12.6 million of the $15.2 million initial wholesaler stocking was constrained due to demand and return uncertainties.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated \u201cNEREUS\u201d at \u201c$2.5M\u201d and warned that \u201c~$0\u20131M turns the guide into a liability.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The cash balance was $170 million, above the preview's roughly $155 million estimate, and management said funding should last through at least the end of 2027. That was a more reassuring runway statement than the preview's projected cash drawdown and focus on how Vanda would fund 2027.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Cash, equivalents and marketable securities were \u201c$170 million\u201d at June 30, down \u201c$32.3 million from Q1 2026,\u201d with management \u201cstill guiding to funding through at least end of 2027.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated \u201cCash + securities (EOQ)\u201d at \u201c\u2248 $155M,\u201d assuming \u201c~$47M burn,\u201d and described funding before 2027 as the dominant cash question.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Management said operating expenses should start declining by year-end 2026 and fall more substantially in 2027 as Phase 3 programs and supply build-out conclude. The preview highlighted rising launch and clinical spending but did not anticipate this stated expense-decline trajectory.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cVanda expects operating expenses to begin decreasing by end of 2026 and more substantially in 2027\u201d as Phase 3 programs and commercial supply build-out conclude.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said Q2 would include \u201cNEREUS DTC launch spend,\u201d \u201cBYSANTI pre-launch build,\u201d and \u201celevated legal costs,\u201d and identified the opex trajectory as a key metric to watch.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The $150\u2013170 million 2026 sales outlook was framed as combined FNAPT and Basanti sales, rather than the preview's Fanapt-only $150\u2013170 million figure. That leaves less visibility into how much of the range depends on Fanapt versus the new Basanti launch.", "direction": "mixed", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cCombined FNAPT and Basanti 2026 net product sales are guided at $150\u2013170 million,\u201d with the Basanti launch expected in the second half of 2026.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview listed a \u201cFanapt\u201d FY2026 guide of \u201c$150\u2013170M\u201d and said to watch whether BYSANTI would be broken out separately because switching could depress the Fanapt line.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Vanda disclosed that the Hetlios jet-lag application is proceeding to a formal FDA administrative hearing, with a favorable judge recommendation only hoped for in early 2027. The preview did not anticipate this hearing process or its timing.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe Hetlios jet-lag disorder application is proceeding to a formal FDA administrative hearing culminating in December 2026,\u201d with a favorable judge recommendation hoped for in early 2027.", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["FNAPT sales were $36 million versus the preview's $35 million estimate, broadly matching its expected rebound.", "Vanda reiterated full-year 2026 total revenue guidance of $240\u2013290 million, consistent with the preview's base case that the guide would be reaffirmed."]}
{"event_id": "ea_NWN_Q2_2026", "ticker": "NWN", "event_datetime": "2026-08-05T15:00:00+00:00", "car1": 0.01600600681124309, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Management raised its 2026 EPS outlook to the top half of the $2.95\u2013$3.15 range, rather than simply reaffirming the range as the preview\u2019s base case anticipated. The preview identified an upper-half outlook as a bullish outcome.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "Management \u201craised its expectation to deliver 2026 EPS in the top half of its $2.95 to $3.15 guidance range,\u201d citing strong year-to-date performance and improved second-half visibility.", "expectation_evidence": "The preview\u2019s base case assumed guidance would be \u201creaffirmed unchanged at $2.95\u2013$3.15\u201d; it called narrowing to the upper half \u201cthe single most bullish outcome available.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 EPS was $0.01 and above expectations, instead of the loss-centered result the preview anticipated. The preview\u2019s adjusted estimate was approximately $0.00 and its consensus midpoint was about a $0.06 loss.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cQ2 2026 total EPS was $0.01, flat year-over-year \u2026 but above expectations.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview put adjusted EPS at \u201c~$0.00,\u201d with consensus GAAP EPS centered around \u201c$(0.06),\u201d and described the base case as an \u201cin-line seasonal loss.\u201d", "expectation_source": "call"}, {"description": "Northwest Natural Gas EPS came in far above the preview\u2019s estimate, at $0.09 rather than $0.01. This was a notable offset to the weaker results in SiEnergy and Water.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNorthwest Natural Gas EPS declining to $0.09 from $0.12.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated NWN Gas Utility EPS of \u201c$0.01\u201d for Q2 2026.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "SiEnergy EPS was $0.05, below the preview\u2019s $0.07 estimate, despite strong customer growth. Its customer growth itself was broadly in line with the preview\u2019s expectations.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cSiEnergy rising to $0.05 from $0.03.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated SiEnergy EPS of \u201c$0.07\u201d (range \u201c$0.06 \u2013 $0.09\u201d).", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Water EPS fell to $0.05, below the preview\u2019s $0.07 estimate. The call also said first-half Water results were modestly below plan.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cWater falling to $0.05 from $0.07\u201d; Water customer growth was 3.4% for the 12 months ended June 30, \u201cthough first-half results were modestly below plan.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview estimated NWN Water EPS of \u201c$0.07\u201d (range \u201c$0.06 \u2013 $0.09\u201d).", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "MX3 is now fully contracted under 25-year agreements, adding a concrete commercial milestone beyond the progress the preview was watching for. The forecast increase in long-term growth remains conditional on Notice to Proceed, which is still expected at the end of 2027.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "The \u201c$300 million FERC-regulated MX3 storage expansion is fully contracted with 25-year agreements at a 12.5% ROE and 50% equity structure\u201d; upon Notice to Proceed, expected at the end of 2027, it \u201cwould raise the long-term earnings growth target from 4%-6% to 5%-7%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said to watch MX3 EPC/permit progress and whether NTP remained \u201cby late 2027,\u201d the trigger for moving long-term growth from \u201c4\u20136% to 5\u20137%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Quarter-end liquidity was about $628 million, above the $590 million figure in the preview. The company also kept planned ATM equity within the expected $40\u2013$50 million range, so the higher liquidity did not come with a reported increase in equity issuance.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "The capital plan includes \u201c$40 million to $50 million of ATM equity, with liquidity of about $628 million at quarter end.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview listed \u201c$590M liquidity\u201d and an \u201cATM plan ($40\u201350M equity in 2026),\u201d and flagged equity above plan as a concern.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The company completed an inaugural $75 million Water bond issuance after receiving an A- investment-grade rating, an additional financing and credit milestone beyond the preview\u2019s expected $70 million senior-notes closing. The financing amount was modestly higher than the preview\u2019s cited debt transaction.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company completed its inaugural $75 million Water bond issuance after receiving an A- investment-grade rating.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said \u201c$70M notes closing today (Aug 5)\u201d and identified financing and the balance sheet as key items to watch.", "expectation_source": "preview"}], "in_line": ["SiEnergy\u2019s reported organic customer growth of over 15% was in line with the preview\u2019s roughly 16% estimate and the 15%\u201320% growth target; its backlog of over 260,000 future meters was somewhat above the preview\u2019s 250,000-plus expectation.", "Washington\u2019s new rates took effect August 1, consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that the revenue increase would be effective in August.", "The Oregon alternative rate mechanism settlement retained the expected modest-increase timing, with new rates expected October 31, 2026. The $13 million settlement was below the company\u2019s original $15.6 million ask, but the preview did not specify an expected settlement amount.", "The $500\u2013$550 million capital expenditure plan and $40\u2013$50 million ATM equity funding were in line with the preview\u2019s expectations.", "MX3\u2019s end-of-2027 Notice to Proceed expectation and the conditional 4%\u20136% to 5%\u20137% long-term growth target change were consistent with the preview\u2019s discussion."]}
{"event_id": "ea_NCNO_Q2_2027", "ticker": "NCNO", "event_datetime": "2026-08-25T20:30:00+00:00", "car1": 0.03342611793435535, "model": "gpt-6-luna", "effort": "medium", "surprises": [{"description": "Q2 non-GAAP operating income was $40.8 million, well above the roughly $37 million consensus and the $36.5 million guidance midpoint. The 25% margin also exceeded the roughly 23% margin implied by guidance.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cNon-GAAP operating income was $40.8 million (25% of total revenues), up 36% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cNon-GAAP operating income\u201d guidance midpoint of \u201c$36.5M\u201d; consensus \u201c~$37M\u201d; implied margin \u201c~23.0% at mid.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Subscription revenue reached $143.5 million, about $2 million above the guide midpoint and guide-anchored consensus, with 10% reported growth versus the expected 8%. Growth excluding U.S. mortgage was 12%, contributing about $1.3 million of upside to guidance.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cSubscription revenues of $143.5 million, up 10% year-over-year\u201d; excluding U.S. mortgage, subscription revenues \u201cgrew 12% year-over-year, delivering approximately $1.3 million of upside to subscription revenue guidance.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Q2 subscription guidance was \u201c$140.25\u2013142.25M\u201d with consensus \u201c~$141.5M\u201d; the preview described the implied growth as \u201c~+8% YoY.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Total revenue was $161 million, above the $159.14 million consensus and the $158.75 million company-guidance midpoint. Growth was 8%, rather than the 6.8% implied by consensus.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cTotal revenues for Q2 FY27 were $161 million, up 8% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "Total revenue consensus was \u201c$159.14M,\u201d guidance midpoint \u201c$158.75M,\u201d and implied year-over-year growth \u201c+6.8%.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Q2 free cash flow was $34 million, a much stronger result than the preview\u2019s expectation of a soft quarter. The preview\u2019s full-year bridge, with $80.8 million already generated in Q1 and roughly $55\u201360 million expected in H2, implied little Q2 contribution; the actual quarter added $34 million.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "large", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFree cash flow reached $34 million, up 170% year-over-year.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "\u201cCollections are heavily front-loaded; H2 FCF of ~$55\u201360M is the plan, so a soft Q2 FCF quarter is not automatically a red flag.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The U.S. mortgage outlook worsened beyond the preview\u2019s expected roughly 2% Q2 decline and approximately 1% full-year growth assumption: nCino cut its Q3 and Q4 mortgage subscription outlook by about $700,000 and $1.2 million, respectively, citing higher IMB churn from persistently high rates. Total company churn, however, was unchanged at about $25 million.", "direction": "negative", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cLowered its U.S. mortgage subscription revenue outlook by approximately $700,000 in Q3 and $1.2 million in Q4 due to higher IMB churn from persistently high mortgage rates, though total company churn remains unchanged at about $25 million.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said U.S. mortgage was \u201cGuided to decline ~2% in Q2\u201d and that \u201c~1% [was] assumed for the full year\u201d; it flagged whether that full-year assumption held as a key issue.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "The platform-pricing transition advanced more quickly than the preview\u2019s concern about slow progress suggested: 48% of ACV was on the new model, up from 40% last quarter, and 12 of the top 20 U.S. enterprise customers had transitioned.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cApproximately 48% of total ACV is now on the new platform pricing model, up from 40% last quarter, and 12 of the top 20 U.S. enterprise customers have transitioned.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview said \u201c>40% of ACV had converted\u201d as of Q1 and noted that this was \u201conly ~2 points of progress in a quarter, slower than the narrative implies.\u201d", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "Four top-20 U.S. enterprise customers renewed early, with an average ACV increase above 10%. This offered favorable renewal and expansion evidence amid the preview\u2019s concern about ACV growth slowing from 17% to roughly 10%, although the call facts do not provide an H1 net-add figure.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "moderate", "fact_evidence": "\u201cFour of the top 20 U.S. enterprise customers (representing over $900 billion in assets) renewed early with an average ACV increase of more than 10%.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "The preview called deceleration from \u201c17% \u2192 10% ACV growth\u201d the \u201csingle most important number\u201d and flagged ACV and net adds as key items to watch.", "expectation_source": "preview"}, {"description": "nCino announced that it had signed what it expects to be its largest deal of the year, an international customer deal, early in Q3. The preview did not anticipate this specific booking.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cThe company signed what it expects to be its largest deal of the year, an international customer deal, in early Q3 FY27.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}, {"description": "The call reported stronger evidence of AI adoption in production: year-to-date Banking Advisor production deployments more than doubled, and one-third of named AI-adopting customers were in production. The preview had focused on IU buyers and usage but did not forecast these production-deployment metrics; no IU revenue figure was provided.", "direction": "positive", "magnitude": "small", "fact_evidence": "\u201cOver 230 customers have purchased AI intelligence units, with year-to-date production deployments of banking advisor more than doubling, and one-third of named AI-adopting customers now in production.\u201d", "expectation_evidence": "", "expectation_source": "none"}], "in_line": ["The company raised FY27 non-GAAP operating income guidance; the preview\u2019s base case expected a mechanical raise roughly commensurate with the quarter\u2019s beat.", "The company raised FY27 free cash flow guidance, as the preview said a raise was available and worth watching.", "The Board authorized an additional $100 million buyback, in line with the preview\u2019s expectation that a new authorization was a live possibility.", "AI intelligence-unit commentary remained without a disclosed revenue figure, consistent with the preview\u2019s expectation that commentary could remain qualitative.", "Professional-services revenue implied by the reported total and subscription figures was about $17.5 million, in line with the preview\u2019s estimate of roughly $17.5 million."]}
